  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "freezer keeps beeping"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "A persistent beep on a fridge-freezer is usually an alarm triggered by one of two things: either a door hasn't sealed properly (letting warm air in), or the appliance has detected that the temperature inside is too high. It could also be a faulty sensor, but let's rule out the simple causes first. Please check if both doors are shut firmly with nothing blocking them, and then put your hand inside to see if it feels genuinely warm rather than just cool."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "the bottom freezer drawer wasn't pushed in properly last night, a bag was sticking out. i've shoved it shut now. fridge feels cold, freezer too I think"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "That’s good news. The alarm was probably triggered by the drawer being left open, which let warm air in and raised the internal temperature. Now that it is shut properly, the appliance should reset itself within a few minutes. Just make sure nothing is blocking the seal when you close it next time to prevent this from happening again. Is there anything else I can help you with?\n[Helpful to see: image \"Fridge not cooling — simple checks first\" — Before suspecting the sealed system, check the temperature setting, that the door seal closes cleanly, that there's air space and clean condenser coils at the back, and listen for the compressor. Unplug before cleaning at the back. A sealed-system or refrigerant repair is a job for a qualified engineer. Typical layout — your model may differ.]"
  }
 ],
